## 4. Empirical Models

Each description \(D\) induces an empirical model,

\[
E(D),
\]

which specifies what can be observed and predicted.

An empirical model may be written as

\[
E(D)
=
\left(
\mathcal{C},
\mathcal{A},
\mathcal{O},
P_D
\right),
\]

where:

- \(\mathcal{C}\) is a set of contexts,
- \(\mathcal{A}\) is a set of actions or interventions,
- \(\mathcal{O}\) is a set of outcomes,
- \(P_D\) is a probability assignment,

\[
P_D:
\mathcal{C}\times\mathcal{A}
\rightarrow
\mathrm{Prob}(\mathcal{O}).
\]

The basic empirical object is therefore

\[
P_D(o\mid a,C).
\]

Two descriptions \(D\) and \(D'\) are empirically equivalent if there exists an isomorphism of empirical models,

\[
E(D)
\cong
E(D'),
\]

preserving all intervention-outcome probabilities:

\[
P_D(o\mid a,C)
=
P_{D'}(o'\mid a',C').
\]

Thus empirical equivalence is the first layer of Omega equivalence.

