## 5. Admissible Transformations

Omega Relativity defines physical equivalence through a broad class of admissible transformations.

Let \(\mathcal{G}\) be the groupoid of admissible transformations.

The equivalence relation \(\sim\) is generated by:

### 5.1 Gauge Transformations

\[
A_\mu
\rightarrow
A_\mu^g.
\]

Gauge-related configurations represent the same physical state.

### 5.2 Coordinate Changes

\[
x^\mu
\rightarrow
x'^\mu(x).
\]

Coordinate-related metrics represent the same geometry.

### 5.3 Dualities

\[
T
\cong
T'.
\]

Dual theories represent the same physical content.

### 5.4 Coarse-Grainings

\[
D_{\text{micro}}
\rightarrow
D_{\text{macro}}.
\]

Effective descriptions related by renormalization or thermodynamic coarse-graining may share invariant content.

### 5.5 Categorical Equivalences

\[
\mathcal{C}
\simeq
\mathcal{C}'.
\]

Equivalent categories of processes represent the same structural content.

### 5.6 Computational Intertranslatability

Two descriptions are equivalent if they can be efficiently or faithfully translated into one another as computational models.

### 5.7 Observer-Frame Transformations

\[
\rho_S^{(Q)}
\rightarrow
\rho_S^{(Q')}.
\]

Descriptions relative to different quantum reference frames are equivalent when related by the appropriate transformation.

### 5.8 Intervention-Context Transformations

\[
P(o\mid \mathrm{do}(I),C)
\rightarrow
P(o'\mid \mathrm{do}(I'),C').
\]

Operational contexts may be transformed without changing invariant empirical structure.

### 5.9 Semantic Reinterpretations

Different meanings may be assigned to the same formalism, or different formalisms may yield the same empirical model.

### 5.10 Reflexive Self-Redescriptions

The principle of equivalence itself may be redescribed, provided the redescription is idempotent:

\[
R^2=R.
\]

Thus the equivalence relation includes its own self-application.

