## 7. Invariant Content

The invariant content of a description \(D\) is the set of structures preserved under all admissible transformations.

Let \(\mathrm{Aut}(D)\) be the group or groupoid of admissible automorphisms of \(D\).

The invariant algebra is

\[
\mathrm{Inv}(D)
=
\left\{
O
\mid
g(O)=O
\ \forall g\in\mathrm{Aut}(D)
\right\}.
\]

Examples include:

1. gauge-invariant observables,
2. diffeomorphism-invariant relational observables,
3. duality-invariant correlation functions,
4. renormalization-group fixed-point data,
5. anomaly coefficients,
6. topological invariants,
7. entanglement spectra,
8. causal order where operationally accessible,
9. thermodynamic equations of state,
10. complexity scaling classes.

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Physical reality is the invariant algebra of admissible description.}
}
\]

---

## 8. The Omega Invariant

The Omega invariant is the total invariant content across all self-consistent, mutually composable descriptions.

Let \(\mathcal{D}_{\text{sc}}\) be the class of self-consistent descriptions.

Let \(\mathcal{G}\) be the groupoid of admissible transformations.

Then the Omega invariant may be written as

\[
\mathcal{R}_\Omega
=
\mathrm{Inv}_{\mathcal{G}}
\left(
\mathcal{D}_{\text{sc}}
\right).
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\mathcal{R}_\Omega
=
\mathcal{D}_{\text{sc}}/\sim.
\]

More categorically, one may write

\[
\mathcal{R}_\Omega
=
\operatorname*{colim}_{D\in\mathcal{D}_{\text{sc}}}
E(D),
\]

where the colimit is taken over admissible translations, dualities, coarse-grainings, and reinterpretations.

This expresses the idea that reality is what is preserved under the total network of consistent descriptions.

