## 9. Self-Consistency

Not every description is admissible.

A description must be self-consistent.

Self-consistency includes:

1. logical consistency,
2. probabilistic normalization,
3. causal consistency,
4. unitarity or appropriate generalization,
5. anomaly cancellation,
6. absence of uncontrolled infinities,
7. stability under refinement,
8. compatibility with empirical data,
9. composability with other descriptions,
10. reflexive stability under self-application.

Thus Omega Relativity is not “anything goes.”

It is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Only self-consistent, composable descriptions contribute to reality.}
}
\]

---

## 10. Mutual Composability

Descriptions must not merely be individually consistent. They must be mutually composable.

Two descriptions \(D_1\) and \(D_2\) are composable if there exists a consistent joint description,

\[
D_1\otimes D_2,
\]

or a consistent gluing,

\[
D_1\cup_\Sigma D_2,
\]

along a common interface \(\Sigma\).

Composability requires:

1. compatible observables,
2. compatible probability rules,
3. compatible causal structures,
4. compatible symmetries,
5. compatible domains of validity,
6. consistent gluing of boundary data.

Thus reality is not a random collection of descriptions. It is a composable network of descriptions.

The Omega invariant is the invariant content of that network.

