## Appendix A: Quotient Construction

Let \(\mathcal{D}\) be the class of descriptions.

Let \(\sim\) be the equivalence relation generated by admissible transformations.

The quotient is

\[
\mathcal{R}
=
\mathcal{D}/\sim.
\]

Each physical entity is an equivalence class,

\[
[D]
=
\{D'\in\mathcal{D}\mid D'\sim D\}.
\]

This generalizes gauge orbits, diffeomorphism classes, duality classes, and universality classes.

---

## Appendix B: Empirical Equivalence

Two descriptions \(D\) and \(D'\) are empirically equivalent if there exists a bijection between their contexts, actions, and outcomes preserving probabilities:

\[
P_D(o\mid a,C)
=
P_{D'}(o'\mid a',C').
\]

Thus empirical content is invariant under empirical equivalence.

---

## Appendix C: Invariant Algebra

For a description \(D\) with automorphism groupoid \(\mathrm{Aut}(D)\), the invariant algebra is

\[
\mathrm{Inv}(D)
=
\left\{
O
\mid
g(O)=O
\ \forall g\in\mathrm{Aut}(D)
\right\}.
\]

Physical observables belong to this invariant algebra.

---

## Appendix D: Reflexive Fixed Point

Let \(R\) be the relativization operation.

Reflexive Relativity requires

\[
R^2=R.
\]

The Omega invariant satisfies

\[
R(\mathcal{R}_\Omega)
=
\mathcal{R}_\Omega.
\]

Thus the framework is stable under self-application.

---

## Appendix E: Symbolic Integral over Descriptions

One may write symbolically,

\[
\mathcal{R}_\Omega
=
\int_{\mathcal{D}/\sim}
\mathcal{D}[D]\,
\delta[\text{consistency}]
\,
\delta[\text{composability}]
\,
\mathrm{Inv}(D).
\]

This expresses the integral character of Omega Relativity.

It is not necessarily a literal path integral. It is a formal representation of quotienting over all admissible descriptions.

